  "chain": "inadequate belt-replacement/tensioning procedure guidance [cause TBD] -> improper belt tension/sheave alignment at June 25 2019 install -> inner belt tensile break after ~10 hrs run-time -> EES B train inoperable (~246 days); parallel: same procedural inadequacy -> as-found tensions outside acceptance criteria after Feb 22 replacement -> continued EES B inoperability -> concurrent brief overlap with EES A out for filter testing on Feb 12 2020 -> both EES trains unavailable",
  "golden_questions": [
    "Q1",
    "Q3",
    "Q4",
    "Q14"
  ],
  "notes": "Form-366 cause_code/category were provided as TBD/provisional; narrative does not state an official NRC cause-code letter, only a root-cause discussion pointing to inadequate procedural guidance for belt tensioning/alignment during maintenance. Two distinct failure episodes are modeled: (1) original June 2019 belt install with inadequate tensioning leading to tensile break Feb 18 2020, and (2) Feb 22 2020 replacement also found with as-found tensions outside acceptance criteria, requiring further correction through Feb 26 2020 - both trace to the same underlying procedural cause. EES A train was operable throughout except a brief 3hr6min outage for filter testing on Feb 12, 2020, which is modeled as a distinct escalation consequence (both trains unavailable) rather than a BACKED_UP_BY edge, since it was not a standing backup during the B-train outage. No corrective action explicitly tied to writing/revising the belt replacement procedure was described beyond adding the tension-check task, which is captured as ca_addtask_tension."
